[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Playwright Architecture vs Selenium and Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right end-to-end testing framework requires a deep understanding of its underlying architecture. In this tutorial, we will examine how Playwright's modern architecture compares to the architectures of Selenium and Cypress."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Selenium Architecture: The WebDriver Protocol"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selenium relies on the JSON Wire Protocol or W3C WebDriver specification. The test script runs outside the browser, sending HTTP requests to a browser driver (like ChromeDriver or GeckoDriver), which then communicates with the browser."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Supports almost every browser and language."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: High latency because every command is an independent HTTP request over the network. Prone to flaky tests due to network timing issues."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Cypress Architecture: Running Inside the Browser"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cypress takes a radically different approach by executing the test code directly inside the browser alongside the application. It runs inside a Node.js server that proxies network traffic."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Extremely fast execution and native access to DOM elements without serialization overhead."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Operates within the browser's security limitations. Struggles with multi-tab testing, multi-domain testing, and non-JavaScript language bindings."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Playwright Architecture: WebSockets and Browser Contexts"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Playwright drives browsers using modern debugging protocols (Chrome DevTools Protocol for Chromium, and custom protocols for Firefox and WebKit). Communication happens over persistent WebSockets, eliminating HTTP request overhead."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Out-of-process architecture enables native multi-tab, multi-origin, and multi-user scenarios. Fast execution speeds comparable to Cypress."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Requires learning a new paradigm if coming strictly from a Selenium background."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example in Playwright"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is a simple test demonstrating Playwright's native handling of multiple contexts and pages, which is difficult or impossible in other frameworks."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('multi-context user simulation', async ({ browser }) => {
  // Create two isolated browser contexts (like separate incognito windows)
  const context1 = await browser.newContext();
  const context2 = await browser.newContext();

  const page1 = await context1.newPage();
  const page2 = await context2.newPage();

  await page1.goto('https://example.com');
  await page2.goto('https://example.com');

  await expect(page1).toHaveTitle('Example Domain');
  await expect(page2).toHaveTitle('Example Domain');
});"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Selenium remains a legacy standard and Cypress offers great DX for single-page apps, Playwright's WebSocket-driven architecture and native multi-context isolation make it the most robust choice for modern web applications."}]
